<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yellow River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yellow River is the second-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flowing from the Tibetan plateau across the north China plain to the Bohai Sea. Chinese civilisation is considered to have begun along it, but it is notorious for devastating floods and changes of course. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qapaghan-qaghan">Qapaghan Qaghan</a> crossed it repeatedly in raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, and its floods of 1344 contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="Yellow River" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: fading · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_River_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zu Ti recovered much of the land south of the <b>Yellow River</b> in the 310s, and Huan Wen, the dominant general of the mid-century, conquered the Cheng Han state in Sichuan in 347, reached the outskirts of Chang'an against the Former Qin in 354 and retook Luoyang in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>North China was a rich agricultural region, but war, land seizures and the <b>Yellow River</b>, which broke its banks and changed course in 1194, brought hardship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1004 the Empress Dowager Chengtian and Emperor Shengzong led an army deep into Song territory to Chanyuan, near the <b>Yellow River</b>, forcing the treaty that brought more than a century of peace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol-led Yuan had been weakened by internal strife, fiscal mismanagement, devastating <b>Yellow River</b> floods and widespread uprisings, and regional warlords and rebel leaders seized power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 422–423 Emperor Mingyuan took Luoyang, Xuchang and Hulao and most of Henan and western Shandong; Emperor Wen's great invasion of 450 was lured across the <b>Yellow River</b>, flanked and destroyed, and Wei armies laid waste the provinces to the south before withdrawing in January 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Shang Yang the state set out to maximise the area under cultivation, clearing most of the forest of the <b>Yellow River</b> valley, dividing the land into household-sized allotments and forcibly relocating people to work them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
